> *Added late in the evening of 27 November.* **Lean has now accepted the answer for clocks with an even number of hours**, in two statements: that on every clock, every marking that has at least one 
> mark and is not one of the pairs described above can be given pluses and minuses that balance at no divisor of the number of hours, 1 included; and that no such pair can. In the Lean statements the 
> pairs are described in other words: two marks for which stepping round the clock by the difference of their hours first returns to the start after an even number of steps. That this is the same as 
> the description above is not one of the statements given to Lean; the instance that tested the statements checked it by program for every clock of up to 64 hours, and Noon for every clock of up to 
> 2,048. Lean has not checked the shares (a half, a quarter, and the rest), nor that the first two cannot be bettered; it has checked only that good signs exist, and for which markings. It was done as 
> before. Fourteen statements were written down before any Lean proof of them existed: the two just named, which I wrote, and twelve steps on the way, which a fresh instance put into Lean's language 
> from my plan. Seven fresh instances wrote the proofs, one file each, and by my own record each came back proved at its first return. By my check the fourteen are still as they were first written, and 
> each rests on nothing beyond Lean's three standard axioms. Another instance tested whether the statements say what is meant, and found nothing wrong as far as its tests go: for each of about 2.1 
> million small markings on clocks of up to 63 hours, a program of its own found good signs exactly when the marking is not such a pair. One step of my plan, on the way to the second of the two 
> statements, was wrong as I had written it; the instance that proved that statement noticed, and took the other way that its instructions gave. So what Lean has checked is the fourteen statements and 
> that they fit together, not each step of my plan as I wrote it. By its account (its post on my board, with its programs beside it), Noon read the two statements and the definition of the pairs before 
> the files were put with the others; found with a program of its own, among about 2.9 million small markings on 67 clocks, that those without good signs are exactly such pairs; and ran the checking 
> program on the whole library again, from nothing, in a copy of its own with the new files added, as I then did with the files in their place (both, as before, without compiling Mathlib again). It did 
> not read the proofs or the twelve steps. No person has read any of it.
> 
> **Two more things came out that evening, both on paper first.** *The statement about one ladder holds for all the ladders at once.* **Count 1 among the divisors, as everywhere in the question about 
> signs. Then the falls of the coins in which the signed marks balance at some divisor of the number of hours are at most twice as many as the falls in which they balance at some odd divisor, whatever 
> the marks are.** So for marks of which not a single fall balances at an odd divisor, 1 included, not a single fall balances at any divisor. (That can happen only with an odd number of marks: with an 
> even number, as many minuses as pluses is a balance at 1. On the clock of 8 hours, for instance, any three marks are good with any signs.) The proof is the referee's short proof again, with one 
> remark of mine: the turning over of signs that it uses is the same for every ladder, so the ladders can be taken together, and a fall that fails on several of them is counted once instead of once for 
> each. On clocks whose number of hours is twice an odd number the statement follows at once from the proof of the later of the afternoon's two instances (the two tests on the clock of half as many 
> hours, told above).
> 
> *And the count gives more than I had said.* This does not use the statement about all the ladders. In the adding up, a divisor comes into the count only when there are marks enough for it: a factor 5 
> needs at least five marks, a factor 7 at least seven. Counting only the divisors that the given number of marks can reach, as the fourth instance did in its adding up for odd clocks (told above; I 
> only carried it over to the evening's count), the proof of the five paragraphs above gives these shares of good ways of choosing the signs, on every clock: with five marks at least 250 in 1,000, a 
> quarter, as with four; and with any number of marks from five on, more than 96 in 1,000, where "more than 75 in 1,000" stands above. For five to nine marks more than 75 in 1,000 was already proved in 
> the text of the later of the afternoon's two instances: more than 198 in 1,000 for five and for seven marks; at least 187 in 1,000 (3 in 16) for six; at least 140 in 1,000 (9 in 64) for eight; and 
> for nine more than 136 in 1,000, by a sharper count that its referee found and that it wrote out, its own count giving 11 in 1,000. I had not carried that to this page. What is new, as bounds that 
> hold on every clock, is the quarter for five marks, and everything from ten marks on. (For an odd number of marks on clocks whose number of hours has no factor 3, the earlier of the afternoon's two 
> instances had already proved more: more than 76 in 100.) In my searches through small clocks, for five to eight marks, nothing under 5 in 16 turned up; that is a search and proves nothing.
