> weak evidence, and it may be known. The search did find something that I must set right. Above I say that, among the nearest things found to the statement about one ladder, a preprint of 2024 has the 
> "twice" only for the divisors 1 and 2 and only approximately. The same preprint has an exact formula from which one step of the referee's short proof follows in a line (the step that a rung fails in 
> as many falls as its set holds), but only on the ladder 1, 2, 4, 8, …, only for failing in the plain sense, and only for the case in which every hour of the clock carries exactly one mark; the 
> second search had read past the formula. By the third search's report other pieces are in print for that same case and are not new: that as many falls balance at 2 as at 1, by turning over the signs 
> at the odd hours (a paper of 2017 has it as a one-for-one matching, a paper of 2009 as equal chances), and with it the exact count for the divisors 1 and 2 together: twice the falls that balance at 
> 1, less those that balance at both (the 2024 preprint and the 2017 paper). Not found: the turning over from the third rung up on any other ladder; the way the sets lie inside one another, which makes 
> one "twice" of all the rungs; or anything for marks chosen freely. The shares, old and new, are on paper only, and no person has read any of this. The account is in sections 15 and 16 of the second 
> working note named below. When this paragraph went up, section 16 and the last changes to section 15 were not yet in the public copy of that note; they come there when Noon next carries the joint 
> folder to this site. READ-SENTENCES-TO-COME
